/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/cissp.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("cissp", [["cs1",0,1,"A security manager discovers that a proposed marketing initiative will create risk above the organization's stated risk appetite. What should the manager do FIRST?",["Present the risk and options to senior management so the business owner can make an informed decision","Block the initiative until controls reduce the risk","Accept the risk on behalf of the organization","Purchase cyber insurance to transfer the risk"],0,"Security advises; senior management owns risk decisions. Informing them with options is the manager's role.","Outline 1.10",[null,"Blocking the initiative oversteps the security role; security advises on risk, but the business owner decides whether a business initiative proceeds.","A security manager does not have the authority to accept risk above the stated appetite on the organization's behalf; that decision belongs to senior management.","Insurance is one possible response, but choosing it is a management decision made after the risk is presented, not the manager's first step."],3],["cs2",0,1,"Under the ISC2 Code of Ethics, which canon takes precedence when canons conflict?",["Provide diligent and competent service to principals","Protect society, the common good, necessary public trust and confidence, and the infrastructure","Advance and protect the profession","Act honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly and legally"],1,"The canons are in priority order, and protecting society comes first.","Outline 1.1",["Service to principals is the third canon, so it yields to protecting society and to acting honorably when they conflict.",null,"Advancing and protecting the profession is the fourth and lowest-priority canon.","Acting honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly and legally is the second canon; it ranks below protecting society."],1],["cs3",0,1,"An asset worth $200,000 has an exposure factor of 25% and is expected to be hit once every four years. What is the annualized loss expectancy?",["$50,000","$200,000","$12,500","$800,000"],2,"SLE = 200,000 x 0.25 = 50,000; ARO = 0.25; ALE = 50,000 x 0.25 = 12,500.","Outline 1.10",["$50,000 is the single loss expectancy (200,000 x 0.25); it still has to be multiplied by the ARO of 0.25 to get the ALE.","$200,000 is the asset value; it ignores both the 25% exposure factor and the once-in-four-years rate.",null,"$800,000 comes from multiplying by 4 instead of by the ARO of 0.25 (one in four years)."],3],["cs4",0,1,"Which activity BEST demonstrates due diligence before signing a contract with a new cloud provider?",["Encrypting data after migration","Signing the contract with a standard SLA","Training staff on the new service","Reviewing the provider's SOC 2 Type II report and security practices"],3,"Due diligence is researching and understanding risk before acting; reviewing assurance reports does that.","Outline 1.3",["Encrypting data after migration is a protective control (due care) applied after the decision, not research done before signing.","Signing with a standard SLA commits to the provider without first investigating its security, which is the opposite of due diligence.","Staff training on the service happens after adoption and addresses usage, not the provider's risk before contracting.",null],2],["cs5",0,1,"When developing a business continuity plan, what should be done FIRST?",["Obtain senior management support and define the scope","Conduct a business impact analysis","Select an alternate processing site","Test the recovery procedures"],0,"Without management backing and a defined scope, the BIA and later steps lack authority and resources.","Outline 1.8",[null,"The BIA is a core early step, but it needs management's mandate and a defined scope first to have authority and resources.","An alternate site is a recovery strategy chosen after the BIA sets RTOs, so it comes much later in planning.","Testing recovery procedures is one of the last steps; there is nothing to test until the plan has been written."],2],["cs6",0,1,"Which document is MANDATORY and specifies a particular technology or configuration required across the organization?",["Guideline","Standard","Policy","Baseline recommendation"],1,"Standards are mandatory and specific; policies are high level and guidelines are optional advice.","Outline 1.7",["A guideline is optional, recommended advice, not a mandatory requirement.",null,"A policy is mandatory but high level; it states management intent without naming specific technologies or settings.","A baseline sets minimum configuration for a system type, and calling it a 'recommendation' makes it optional, which contradicts the question."],1],["cs7",0,1,"The maximum amount of time a business process can be unavailable before the organization suffers unacceptable harm is called the:",["Recovery point objective (RPO)","Mean time to repair (MTTR)","Maximum tolerable downtime (MTD)","Work recovery time (WRT)"],2,"MTD is the upper limit; RTO must be set below it.","Outline 1.8",["RPO is the maximum acceptable data loss measured back in time, not how long a process can be down.","MTTR is the average time to repair a component, an operational metric rather than a business tolerance limit.",null,"Work recovery time is the time needed after systems are restored to verify data and resume work; it is one part of the total downtime."],1],["cs8",0,1,"An organization buys cyber insurance to cover losses from a data breach. Which risk response is this?",["Avoidance","Mitigation","Acceptance","Transference"],3,"Insurance shifts the financial impact to a third party, which is risk transfer.","Outline 1.10",["Avoidance means stopping the risky activity altogether; buying insurance keeps the activity running.","Mitigation reduces the likelihood or impact with controls; insurance does not make a breach less likely or less damaging.","Acceptance means knowingly bearing the loss yourself, whereas insurance moves the financial loss to the insurer.",null],1],["cs9",0,1,"Which threat modeling method categorizes threats as spoofing, tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service and elevation of privilege?",["STRIDE","PASTA","DREAD","OCTAVE"],0,"STRIDE is Microsoft's threat categorization model.","Outline 1.11",[null,"PASTA is a seven-stage, risk-centric threat modeling process, not a six-category threat classification.","DREAD rates threats by damage, reproducibility, exploitability, affected users and discoverability; it scores threats rather than categorizing them.","OCTAVE is an organizational risk assessment methodology from Carnegie Mellon, not a threat category mnemonic."],1],["cs10",0,1,"What is the BEST way to reduce the risk of fraud by an employee who controls both vendor creation and payment approval?",["Increase the employee's background check depth","Separate those duties between different employees","Require annual ethics training","Install cameras in the accounting office"],1,"Separation of duties prevents one person from completing a fraudulent transaction alone.","Outline 1.9",["Deeper background checks screen people at hiring but do not stop one trusted person from both creating and paying a fake vendor.",null,"Ethics training raises awareness but is not a control that prevents a single person from completing the fraud.","Cameras can deter physical misconduct but cannot see or stop fraudulent vendor records created in a system."],2],["cs11",0,1,"A company wants to ensure its security program supports the organization's goals. What is MOST important?",["Adopting the latest security technologies","Maximizing the number of controls implemented","Aligning security governance with business strategy, goals and mission","Matching the security budget of competitors"],2,"Governance exists to support business objectives, not technology for its own sake.","Outline 1.3",["Adopting new technology for its own sake does not ensure the program supports what the business needs.","More controls is not better; excess controls waste money and can hinder the business without reducing relevant risk.",null,"Competitors' budgets reflect their risks and goals, not yours, so matching them does not align security with your strategy."],3],["cs12",0,1,"Personal data is being transferred from the EU to a processor in another country. What should the security professional ensure FIRST?",["That the data is encrypted with AES-256","That the processor has a firewall","That the transfer is logged in the SIEM","That a lawful transfer mechanism, such as an adequacy decision or standard contractual clauses, is in place"],3,"Transborder data flow is a legal requirement first; technical controls come after legal compliance.","Outline 1.4",["AES-256 protects confidentiality, but encryption alone does not make an international transfer lawful under GDPR.","A firewall at the processor is a technical control; the legal basis for the transfer must be established first.","Logging the transfer records that it happened but does nothing to make it compliant.",null],3],["cs13",0,1,"Which is the MOST important measure of a security awareness program's effectiveness?",["A reduction in security incidents caused by user behavior","Percentage of staff completing training","Number of training modules produced","Positive employee feedback surveys"],0,"Effectiveness is shown by changed behavior and outcomes, not completion counts.","Outline 1.13",[null,"Completion rates show people attended training, not that their behavior changed.","Counting modules produced measures program output, not whether users act more securely.","Satisfaction surveys show people liked the training, which does not prove fewer risky actions."],2],["cs14",0,1,"When a supplier provides critical software components, which control BEST addresses supply chain risk?",["Rejecting all third-party software","Contractual security requirements with the right to audit and ongoing supplier assessment","Relying on the supplier's marketing materials","Scanning the supplier's website for vulnerabilities once"],1,"Supply chain risk management needs contractual requirements and continuous assessment of the supplier.","Outline 1.12",["Rejecting all third-party software is impractical for almost any organization and avoids the risk only by giving up needed capability.",null,"Marketing materials are self-promotion, not independent evidence of the supplier's security.","A one-time scan of the supplier's website says nothing about the security of the software components it delivers."],2],["cs15",0,2,"Who is ultimately responsible for determining the classification of a data set?",["The data custodian","The system administrator","The data owner","The chief information security officer"],2,"The owner, a senior business manager, is accountable for classifying and protecting the data.","Outline 2.1",["The custodian implements and maintains controls on the owner's behalf but does not decide the classification.","A system administrator typically acts as a custodian; they operate systems but are not accountable for the data's classification.",null,"The CISO sets the classification scheme and advises, but the business owner of each data set assigns its classification."],1],["cs16",0,2,"An organization will reuse solid-state drives that stored confidential data. Which method is MOST appropriate?",["Formatting the drive","Degaussing the drive","Deleting the files and emptying the recycle bin","Cryptographic erase or manufacturer secure erase"],3,"SSDs are not magnetic and wear leveling defeats overwriting; purge via secure or cryptographic erase is appropriate.","Outline 2.4",["Formatting mainly rewrites file system structures; the underlying data remains recoverable, especially on SSDs.","Degaussing works on magnetic media; SSDs store data in flash cells, so degaussing does not erase them (and would ruin a hard drive meant for reuse).","Deleting files and emptying the recycle bin only removes pointers; the data stays on the drive until overwritten.",null],3],["cs17",0,2,"What is the PRIMARY purpose of data classification?",["To apply protection proportional to the data's value and sensitivity","To reduce the number of files stored","To comply with encryption export laws","To identify which employees created the data"],0,"Classification drives consistent, cost-effective protection and handling requirements.","Outline 2.1",[null,"Reducing file counts is a side effect of retention and cleanup, not the reason for classifying data.","Export control laws concern cryptographic products, not the classification of an organization's data.","Tracking who created data is attribution or auditing; classification is about sensitivity and value."],1],["cs18",0,2,"Under GDPR terminology, a payroll company that processes employee data on behalf of a client is the:",["Data controller","Data processor","Data subject","Data owner"],1,"The processor acts on behalf of the controller, who determines purposes and means.","Outline 2.2",["The controller decides the purposes and means of processing; here that is the client whose employees' data it is.",null,"The data subject is the individual the data describes, in this case each employee.","Data owner is an organizational role for accountability, not a GDPR term for a company processing data on another's behalf."],1],["cs19",0,2,"Which role is responsible for day-to-day implementation of the protection controls specified for data?",["Data owner","Data subject","Data custodian","Auditor"],2,"Custodians (often IT) implement and maintain controls on the owner's behalf.","Outline 2.2",["The data owner is accountable and specifies the controls but does not usually implement them day to day.","The data subject is the person the data is about and has no role in operating controls.",null,"An auditor independently checks whether controls work; they do not implement or run them."],1],["cs20",0,2,"An organization adopts a NIST control baseline and removes controls that do not apply to its systems. This is called:",["Tailoring","Baselining","Benchmarking","Scoping"],3,"Scoping removes inapplicable controls; tailoring adjusts the remaining ones.","Outline 2.6",["Tailoring modifies the remaining controls, such as adjusting parameters, to fit the organization; it is not the removal of inapplicable ones.","Baselining is establishing the starting set of controls, not trimming it.","Benchmarking compares your practices or performance to an external reference; it does not remove controls from a baseline.",null],3],["cs21",0,2,"What is the BEST reason to limit data collection to only what is necessary?",["It reduces the impact of a breach and supports privacy compliance","It makes backups faster","It eliminates the need for encryption","It removes the need for classification"],0,"Data minimization lowers exposure and aligns with privacy principles.","Outline 2.3",[null,"Backup speed is a minor operational benefit, not the main reason privacy principles call for minimization.","Collecting less data does not remove the need to encrypt the sensitive data you do keep.","Whatever data is collected still has to be classified so it can be handled correctly."],2],["cs22",0,2,"Which control BEST protects sensitive data while it is being processed in memory?",["Full-disk encryption","Confidential computing using a trusted execution environment","TLS 1.3","Data retention schedules"],1,"Data in use is protected by secure enclaves or TEEs; disk encryption and TLS cover rest and transit.","Outline 2.6",["Full-disk encryption protects data at rest; once data is loaded into memory for processing it is decrypted.",null,"TLS 1.3 protects data in transit over the network, not while an application is processing it in memory.","Retention schedules decide how long data is kept; they provide no protection while data is being processed."],3],["cs23",0,2,"A company holds customer records well beyond any legal or business requirement. What is the GREATEST risk?",["Higher backup licensing costs","Slower database queries","Increased liability and exposure if the data is breached or subject to discovery","Reduced disk space for new projects"],2,"Over-retention increases legal and breach exposure; retention should follow a defined schedule.","Outline 2.4",["Backup licensing costs are a real but minor expense compared with legal and breach exposure.","Query performance is an operational nuisance, not the greatest risk of keeping unneeded personal data.",null,"Disk space is cheap and easily expanded; it is not the main risk of over-retention."],3],["cs24",0,3,"Which security model prevents a subject at the Secret level from writing information to an Unclassified object?",["Biba","Clark-Wilson","Brewer-Nash","Bell-LaPadula"],3,"Bell-LaPadula's star property (no write down) protects confidentiality.","Outline 3.2",["Biba protects integrity, so it prevents writing up to higher-integrity objects, not writing down to lower-classified ones.","Clark-Wilson enforces integrity through well-formed transactions and separation of duties, not classification levels.","Brewer-Nash prevents conflicts of interest between competing clients rather than enforcing classification levels.",null],1],["cs25",0,3,"Which model is designed to prevent conflicts of interest, such as a consultant accessing data of competing clients?",["Brewer-Nash (Chinese Wall)","Bell-LaPadula","Biba","Take-Grant"],0,"Brewer-Nash dynamically restricts access based on previously accessed data sets.","Outline 3.2",[null,"Bell-LaPadula enforces confidentiality by classification level; it does not track conflicts between competing clients.","Biba enforces integrity levels and has no notion of competing data sets.","Take-Grant models how rights are passed between subjects and objects; it is not built around conflict-of-interest classes."],1],["cs26",0,3,"Which Biba rule prevents a high-integrity process from reading low-integrity data?",["Star integrity property (no write up)","Simple integrity property (no read down)","Simple security property (no read up)","Discretionary security property"],1,"Biba's simple integrity property stops reading lower-integrity data that could corrupt the subject.","Outline 3.2",["The star integrity property prevents writing up to higher-integrity objects, not reading lower-integrity data.",null,"No read up is Bell-LaPadula's simple security property for confidentiality, not a Biba integrity rule.","The discretionary security property is Bell-LaPadula's use of an access matrix, not a Biba integrity rule."],1],["cs27",0,3,"Which cryptographic mechanism provides integrity, authentication and non-repudiation of a message?",["Symmetric encryption","Message digest alone","Digital signature","Key escrow"],2,"A signature made with the sender's private key proves origin and integrity and cannot be repudiated.","Outline 3.6",["Symmetric encryption gives confidentiality, but because both parties share the key it cannot prove which one sent a message.","A digest alone detects changes, but anyone can recompute it, so it provides no authentication or non-repudiation.",null,"Key escrow stores copies of keys with a third party for recovery; it does not protect messages."],1],["cs28",0,3,"An attacker measures power consumption of a smart card to recover its key. This is a:",["Brute-force attack","Birthday attack","Replay attack","Side-channel attack"],3,"Side-channel attacks exploit physical emissions like power, timing or electromagnetic leakage.","Outline 3.7",["Brute force tries every possible key mathematically; it does not rely on measuring the device's physical behavior.","A birthday attack exploits hash collision probability and has nothing to do with power measurements.","A replay attack resends captured valid messages; it does not recover keys from physical emissions.",null],2],["cs29",0,3,"A physical door must be designed for the case where power fails. For an emergency exit in an occupied building, what should it do?",["Fail open (fail-safe) to allow people to exit","Fail secure to protect assets","Lock and trigger an alarm","Remain in its last state"],0,"Life safety always takes priority over asset protection.","Outline 3.9",[null,"Fail secure protects assets but could trap occupants during an emergency, and life safety always comes first.","Locking the exit during a power failure puts occupants at risk; an alarm does not let them out.","Remaining in its last state could leave the door locked with people inside, which fails the life-safety requirement."],2],["cs30",0,3,"Which fire suppression system is MOST suitable for a data center to reduce accidental water discharge?",["Wet pipe sprinkler system","Pre-action sprinkler system","Deluge system","Handheld Class A extinguishers only"],1,"Pre-action requires a detection event before the pipes fill, reducing accidental discharge.","Outline 3.9",["Wet pipe systems hold water in the pipes at all times, so a damaged pipe or head discharges water immediately.",null,"Deluge systems open all heads at once and release large volumes of water, which suits high-hazard areas, not data centers.","Class A extinguishers are for ordinary combustibles and are not a fixed suppression system for a whole data center."],2],["cs31",0,3,"What is the MOST important factor in the strength of a cryptographic implementation?",["Secrecy of the algorithm","Length of the ciphertext","Protection and management of the keys","Speed of the encryption"],2,"Kerckhoffs's principle: security should rest on the key, so key management is critical.","Outline 3.6",["Relying on secret algorithms is security through obscurity; Kerckhoffs's principle says a system must stay secure even when the algorithm is public.","Ciphertext length usually tracks plaintext length and says nothing about how hard the encryption is to break.",null,"Speed affects performance, not how resistant the cryptography is to attack."],2],["cs32",0,3,"Which component provides hardware-based secure key storage and platform integrity measurements on a laptop?",["BIOS password","Hypervisor","Memory management unit","Trusted Platform Module (TPM)"],3,"The TPM stores keys and measures boot components for attestation.","Outline 3.4",["A BIOS password restricts firmware settings but provides no secure key storage or boot measurements.","A hypervisor manages virtual machines; it is software and does not provide a hardware root of trust for keys.","The MMU translates and isolates memory addresses; it does not store keys or measure boot integrity.",null],1],["cs33",0,3,"An organization runs industrial control systems that cannot be patched. What is the BEST compensating control?",["Isolating the systems in a segmented network with strict access control and monitoring","Replacing the systems immediately regardless of cost","Installing antivirus on the PLCs","Disabling all logging to improve performance"],0,"Segmentation and monitoring reduce exposure when patching is not possible.","Outline 3.5",[null,"Immediate replacement regardless of cost ignores business impact and is rarely feasible for industrial systems; a compensating control is needed now.","PLCs generally cannot run antivirus, and adding software could affect safety and vendor support.","Disabling logging removes visibility and weakens security; it does nothing to compensate for missing patches."],3],["cs34",0,3,"Which secure design principle is violated when a new application ships with all features and admin interfaces enabled?",["Separation of duties","Secure defaults","Keep it simple","Shared responsibility"],1,"Systems should ship in their most secure configuration, with features enabled only when needed.","Outline 3.1",["Separation of duties splits critical tasks between people; it concerns roles, not default configuration.",null,"Keep it simple (economy of mechanism) is about minimizing design complexity, not which features are on by default.","Shared responsibility describes the split of security duties between a cloud provider and customer."],2],["cs35",0,3,"Two users need to exchange a symmetric key over an untrusted network without prior contact. Which algorithm is designed for this?",["AES","SHA-256","Diffie-Hellman","3DES"],2,"Diffie-Hellman key agreement lets parties establish a shared secret over an insecure channel.","Outline 3.6",["AES is a symmetric cipher that needs a shared key already in place; it cannot establish one on its own.","SHA-256 is a hash function used for integrity; it does not exchange keys.",null,"3DES is a symmetric cipher that also requires a pre-shared key, and it is deprecated."],2],["cs36",0,4,"At which OSI layer does a router make forwarding decisions based on IP addresses?",["Data link layer (Layer 2)","Transport layer (Layer 4)","Session layer (Layer 5)","Network layer (Layer 3)"],3,"IP addressing and routing occur at Layer 3.","Outline 4.1",["Layer 2 devices such as switches forward frames by MAC address, not by IP address.","Layer 4 handles end-to-end transport such as TCP and UDP ports, not routing between networks.","Layer 5 manages sessions between applications and plays no part in packet forwarding.",null],1],["cs37",0,4,"Which protocol suite provides confidentiality and integrity for traffic at the network layer between two sites?",["IPsec","TLS","SSH","SNMPv2c"],0,"IPsec operates at Layer 3 and is the standard for site-to-site VPNs.","Outline 4.1",[null,"TLS protects individual application sessions at the transport layer and above, not all traffic at the network layer.","SSH secures remote sessions and tunnels at the application layer; it is not the standard for network-layer site-to-site protection.","SNMPv2c is a network management protocol with cleartext community strings and provides no confidentiality."],1],["cs38",0,4,"In IPsec, which protocol provides encryption of the payload?",["Authentication Header (AH)","Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)","Internet Key Exchange (IKE)","Generic Routing Encapsulation (GRE)"],1,"ESP provides confidentiality; AH provides integrity and authentication only.","Outline 4.1",["AH provides integrity and origin authentication but does not encrypt the payload.",null,"IKE negotiates security associations and keys; it does not itself encrypt the data packets.","GRE is a tunneling protocol with no encryption; it is often paired with IPsec for that reason."],1],["cs39",0,4,"An organization wants to stop attackers from moving laterally between workloads in its data center. What is the BEST approach?",["Add a larger perimeter firewall","Increase internet bandwidth","Implement micro-segmentation with policy enforced per workload","Deploy a CDN"],2,"Micro-segmentation controls east-west traffic between workloads.","Outline 4.1",["A bigger perimeter firewall controls north-south traffic entering the data center but does not see east-west traffic between workloads.","More bandwidth improves capacity and does nothing to restrict lateral movement.",null,"A CDN caches and delivers public content; it does not control traffic between internal workloads."],3],["cs40",0,4,"Which wireless security configuration is MOST appropriate for a corporate network?",["WPA2-Personal with a strong passphrase","WEP with MAC filtering","An open network with a captive portal","WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X authentication"],3,"Enterprise mode gives per-user authentication and revocation.","Outline 4.1",["WPA2-Personal uses one shared passphrase, so there is no per-user authentication and revoking one user means changing it for everyone.","WEP is cryptographically broken, and MAC addresses are easily spoofed.","An open network has no over-the-air encryption; a captive portal only gates access and does not protect traffic.",null],2],["cs41",0,4,"Which device inspects traffic inline and can automatically block detected attacks?",["Intrusion prevention system","Intrusion detection system","Network tap","Protocol analyzer"],0,"IPS sits inline and blocks; IDS only alerts.","Outline 4.2",[null,"An IDS monitors a copy of traffic and alerts, but it is not inline and cannot block attacks itself.","A network tap only copies traffic to a monitoring tool; it performs no inspection or blocking.","A protocol analyzer captures and decodes packets for troubleshooting but does not block anything."],1],["cs42",0,4,"What is the BEST mitigation for a large volumetric DDoS attack against a public website?",["Adding more RAM to the web server","Contracting an upstream DDoS scrubbing service or CDN","Blocking ICMP at the host firewall","Increasing log retention"],1,"Volumetric attacks saturate links before on-premises controls can help, so mitigation must be upstream.","Outline 4.2",["Extra RAM does not help when the attack floods the network link before traffic reaches the server.",null,"Blocking ICMP on the host does nothing once the inbound link is already saturated, and volumetric floods use many protocols.","Longer log retention helps later analysis but does not mitigate an attack in progress."],2],["cs43",0,4,"Which control verifies a device's health and identity before allowing it onto the network?",["Proxy server","Load balancer","Network access control (NAC)","DNS sinkhole"],2,"NAC checks posture and authentication before granting network access.","Outline 4.2",["A proxy mediates application requests such as web traffic; it does not check device health before network admission.","A load balancer spreads traffic across servers and does not authenticate or assess connecting devices.",null,"A DNS sinkhole redirects lookups for malicious domains; it does not check device posture or identity."],2],["cs44",0,4,"Which protocol should replace Telnet for remote administration?",["FTP","rlogin","SNMPv1","SSH"],3,"SSH encrypts the session, including credentials; Telnet sends them in cleartext.","Outline 4.3",["FTP also sends credentials in cleartext and is for file transfer, not remote administration.","rlogin is a legacy cleartext remote login protocol with weak trust-based authentication, as insecure as Telnet.","SNMPv1 is a management protocol with cleartext community strings, not an encrypted remote shell.",null],1],["cs45",0,4,"Which technology is MOST effective at protecting DNS responses from being forged?",["DNSSEC","DNS over UDP","Split-horizon DNS","Round-robin DNS"],0,"DNSSEC signs DNS records so resolvers can verify authenticity and integrity.","Outline 4.2",[null,"DNS over UDP is simply the normal transport for DNS and adds no authentication of responses.","Split-horizon DNS returns different answers to internal and external clients; it does not stop forged responses.","Round-robin DNS spreads load across several addresses and provides no integrity protection."],2],["cs46",0,4,"A company is connecting a third-party vendor to its network for support. What is the BEST approach?",["Give the vendor a standard employee VPN account","Provide restricted, monitored access to only the required systems under a signed agreement","Allow direct RDP from the internet","Share an administrator password by email"],1,"Third-party connectivity should be least privilege, contractual and monitored.","Outline 4.3",["A standard employee VPN account usually grants broad network access that a vendor does not need and is harder to monitor and revoke.",null,"Direct RDP from the internet exposes systems to brute-force and exploitation and gives no granular control.","Sharing an admin password by email exposes it in transit and storage and removes individual accountability."],2],["cs47",0,4,"Which converged protocol carries SCSI storage commands over IP networks and should be isolated on its own network?",["VoIP","SIP","iSCSI","MPLS"],2,"iSCSI carries storage traffic over IP and should be segmented and authenticated.","Outline 4.1",["VoIP carries voice traffic over IP, not SCSI storage commands.","SIP is a signaling protocol for setting up voice and video calls, not a storage protocol.",null,"MPLS is a label-switching WAN transport technology; it does not carry SCSI commands itself."],1],["cs48",0,5,"An employee transfers from finance to marketing but keeps finance system access. What is this called?",["Privilege escalation","Account takeover","Session hijacking","Privilege creep"],3,"Accumulated access from role changes is privilege creep; access reviews and mover processes prevent it.","Outline 5.5",["Privilege escalation is gaining higher rights through an exploit or misconfiguration, not keeping old rights after a job change.","Account takeover is an attacker gaining control of a legitimate account, which did not happen here.","Session hijacking is stealing an active session token; it has nothing to do with leftover access from a transfer.",null],2],["cs49",0,5,"What is the BEST control to detect inappropriate accumulated access rights?",["Periodic user access reviews by managers or data owners","Longer password requirements","Annual security awareness training","Account lockout thresholds"],0,"Recertification catches access that is no longer needed.","Outline 5.5",[null,"Longer passwords strengthen authentication but do nothing to reveal access a user no longer needs.","Awareness training changes user behavior but does not identify excessive permissions.","Lockout thresholds counter password guessing and do not reveal accumulated rights."],2],["cs50",0,5,"Which access control model assigns permissions based on job function?",["Mandatory access control (MAC)","Role-based access control (RBAC)","Discretionary access control (DAC)","Rule-based access control"],1,"RBAC ties permissions to roles that map to job functions.","Outline 5.4",["MAC grants access by comparing system-enforced labels and clearances, not by job function.",null,"DAC lets data owners grant access at their own discretion rather than by role.","Rule-based access control applies global rules, such as firewall rules or time-of-day limits, to all users regardless of job."],1],["cs51",0,5,"Which model uses security labels and clearances enforced by the system rather than the data owner?",["Discretionary access control (DAC)","Role-based access control (RBAC)","Mandatory access control (MAC)","Attribute-based access control (ABAC)"],2,"In MAC the system enforces label-based decisions; users cannot change them.","Outline 5.4",["In DAC the data owner decides who gets access, which is the opposite of system-enforced labels.","RBAC assigns permissions through roles tied to job functions, not through classification labels and clearances.",null,"ABAC evaluates policies over many attributes; it is not the classic label-and-clearance model the question describes."],1],["cs52",0,5,"A user logs in once and gains access to several web applications from different organizations using SAML. What is this?",["Kerberos realm trust","Password synchronization","Local account mapping","Federated identity"],3,"Federation lets identity from one organization's IdP be trusted by another's service provider.","Outline 5.3",["Kerberos realm trusts link Kerberos domains, typically within an enterprise; they do not use SAML across organizations' web apps.","Password synchronization keeps the same password on several systems, but the user still logs in to each one separately.","Local account mapping ties accounts together within one system and does not give cross-organization single sign-on.",null],2],["cs53",0,5,"Which protocol is an identity layer built on OAuth 2.0?",["OpenID Connect","SAML 2.0","LDAP","RADIUS"],0,"OIDC adds authentication (ID tokens) on top of OAuth 2.0 authorization.","Outline 5.3",[null,"SAML 2.0 is an XML-based federation standard that is separate from OAuth 2.0, not built on it.","LDAP is a directory access protocol, not an identity layer on top of OAuth.","RADIUS is a network AAA protocol for things like VPN and Wi-Fi access and is unrelated to OAuth."],1],["cs54",0,5,"In Kerberos, what does the Key Distribution Center issue after initial authentication?",["A SAML assertion","A ticket-granting ticket","An X.509 certificate","A one-time password"],1,"The authentication service issues a TGT used to request service tickets.","Outline 5.6",["SAML assertions come from a SAML identity provider in web federation, not from a Kerberos KDC.",null,"X.509 certificates are issued by a certificate authority in a PKI, not by the Kerberos KDC.","Kerberos does not issue one-time passwords; OTPs come from token devices or authenticator apps."],1],["cs55",0,5,"When comparing biometric systems, which metric is the BEST single indicator of accuracy?",["False acceptance rate alone","Enrollment time","Crossover error rate (CER)","Throughput rate"],2,"CER is where FAR equals FRR; lower is more accurate.","Outline 5.6",["FAR alone ignores false rejections, so a system can have a low FAR simply by rejecting almost everyone.","Enrollment time affects user acceptance, not how accurately the system matches people.",null,"Throughput is how many people the system can process per minute, a speed measure rather than accuracy."],3],["cs56",0,5,"What is the MOST important step when an employee is terminated for cause?",["Delete the user's mailbox immediately","Collect the badge a week later","Send a company-wide announcement","Disable the user's access at or before the time they are notified"],3,"Access removal must be timely to prevent retaliation; data may need to be retained.","Outline 5.5",["Deleting the mailbox immediately may destroy evidence or records that must be retained; access should be disabled, not the data erased.","Waiting a week to collect the badge leaves physical access open to a possibly disgruntled former employee.","A company-wide announcement is not a priority and can raise privacy or legal concerns; removing access is what protects the organization.",null],2],["cs57",0,5,"Administrators should hold elevated rights only when approved and for a limited time. Which approach does this describe?",["Just-in-time privileged access","Single sign-on","Discretionary access control","Password vaulting without checkout"],0,"JIT removes standing privilege, reducing attack surface.","Outline 5.2",[null,"Single sign-on lets users authenticate once to many systems; it does not limit how long elevated rights last.","DAC lets owners grant access at their discretion and does not provide time-bound, approved elevation.","A vault without checkout leaves standing access to privileged credentials, the opposite of time-limited approval."],2],["cs58",0,5,"Which combination is true multifactor authentication?",["A password and a security question","A password and a hardware token code","A fingerprint and a palm scan","A PIN and a passphrase"],1,"MFA requires different factor types: something you know plus something you have.","Outline 5.2",["A password and a security question are both something you know, so this is single-factor authentication.",null,"A fingerprint and a palm scan are both something you are, so they are the same factor type.","A PIN and a passphrase are both something you know, so this is still single-factor."],2],["cs59",0,5,"Which centralized AAA protocol encrypts the entire payload and separates authentication, authorization and accounting?",["RADIUS","PAP","TACACS+","LDAP"],2,"TACACS+ uses TCP, encrypts the full body and separates AAA functions; RADIUS encrypts only the password.","Outline 5.6",["RADIUS uses UDP, encrypts only the password field and combines authentication with authorization.","PAP sends credentials in cleartext and is only an authentication method, not a centralized AAA protocol.",null,"LDAP is a directory protocol; it does not provide separate accounting or full-payload encryption as an AAA service."],1],["cs60",0,6,"What must be obtained BEFORE beginning a penetration test?",["A list of known vulnerabilities","Administrator passwords for all systems","Approval from the internet service provider","Written authorization from management with authority over the systems"],3,"Testing without written permission may be illegal and violates ethics.","Outline 6.2",["A list of known vulnerabilities may help scoping, but testing without permission is illegal even with that list in hand.","Testers do not need every admin password up front, and credentials do not replace legal authorization.","The ISP does not own the target systems; authorization must come from management who own them.",null],2],["cs61",0,6,"A customer wants assurance that a vendor's controls operated effectively over the past year. Which report should they request?",["SOC 2 Type II","SOC 2 Type I","SOC 1 Type I","SOC 3"],0,"Type II tests operating effectiveness over a period; SOC 2 covers security controls.","Outline 6.5",[null,"SOC 2 Type I assesses the design of controls at a single point in time, not how they operated over the past year.","SOC 1 covers controls relevant to financial reporting, and Type I is only a point-in-time design review.","SOC 3 is a short general-use summary without the detailed test results a customer needs to evaluate operating effectiveness."],2],["cs62",0,6,"Which technique proactively tests an application's availability by running scripted user actions?",["Real user monitoring","Synthetic transactions","Log review","Static code analysis"],1,"Synthetic monitoring simulates users to detect issues before real users are affected.","Outline 6.2",["Real user monitoring passively observes actual user traffic; it does not proactively run scripted actions.",null,"Log review is after-the-fact analysis of recorded events, not proactive testing of availability.","Static analysis examines source code without running the application, so it cannot test availability."],2],["cs63",0,6,"Which testing technique sends malformed or random input to find crashes and unexpected behavior?",["Static analysis","Code review","Fuzzing","Regression testing"],2,"Fuzz testing uses invalid or random input to find robustness issues.","Outline 6.2",["Static analysis inspects code without executing it and does not feed input to a running program.","Code review is a manual inspection of source code, not automated input testing.",null,"Regression testing re-runs existing tests to confirm changes did not break features; it does not generate malformed input."],1],["cs64",0,6,"A penetration tester is given full network diagrams and source code. What type of test is this?",["Black box (zero knowledge)","Gray box (partial knowledge)","Blind test","White box (full knowledge)"],3,"White-box testers have complete information about the target.","Outline 6.2",["A black box tester has no prior knowledge of the target, the opposite of receiving diagrams and code.","A gray box tester gets only partial information, such as user credentials, not full diagrams and source code.","A blind test gives the tester little or no information about the target, though the defenders know a test is happening.",null],1],["cs65",0,6,"What is the PRIMARY purpose of an independent third-party audit?",["To provide objective assurance to stakeholders about controls","To fix vulnerabilities found internally","To replace internal testing","To train the security team"],0,"Independence gives objectivity and credibility to the findings.","Outline 6.1",[null,"Auditors assess and report; fixing vulnerabilities is management's responsibility, and auditors fixing issues would undermine their independence.","External audits complement internal testing but do not replace ongoing internal assessment.","Training the security team is not the purpose of an audit, which is to give stakeholders objective assurance."],2],["cs66",0,6,"After a vulnerability assessment, what should the security manager do FIRST with the results?",["Patch every finding immediately","Prioritize findings by risk to the business and assign remediation owners","Share the raw scan output with all staff","Rerun the scan with more plugins"],1,"Results must be analyzed and risk-ranked so resources go to the most important issues.","Outline 6.4",["Patching every finding immediately ignores risk ranking, wastes effort on low-risk items and can cause outages without testing.",null,"Raw scan output is sensitive, since it maps weaknesses, and should be shared only with people who need it.","Rerunning with more plugins produces more data but does not act on the findings already in hand."],2],["cs67",0,6,"Which metric is a key risk indicator rather than a key performance indicator?",["Number of training sessions delivered","Help desk tickets closed per week","Number of critical vulnerabilities older than 30 days","Firewall uptime percentage"],2,"KRIs signal rising risk exposure; aging critical vulnerabilities indicate growing risk.","Outline 6.3",["Training sessions delivered measures program activity, which is a performance indicator, not a signal of rising risk.","Tickets closed per week measures help desk productivity, a performance indicator.",null,"Firewall uptime measures service availability, a performance indicator rather than a sign of growing risk exposure."],3],["cs68",0,6,"Why should backup restoration be tested regularly?",["To reduce storage costs","To satisfy antivirus requirements","To remove old backup media","To verify data can actually be recovered within the required objectives"],3,"Untested backups may fail when needed; restore tests prove RTO and RPO can be met.","Outline 6.3",["Restore testing uses extra resources; it is not a way to reduce storage costs.","Antivirus has no requirement for backup restore tests.","Retiring old media is part of media management; restore tests verify recoverability, not disposal.",null],2],["cs69",0,6,"Which type of scan gives the MOST accurate view of missing patches on a server?",["Credentialed (authenticated) scan","Unauthenticated external scan","Ping sweep","Banner grab"],0,"Authenticated scans can inspect installed software and configurations directly.","Outline 6.2",[null,"An unauthenticated external scan can only infer patch levels from exposed services and banners, which is far less accurate.","A ping sweep only finds live hosts and reveals nothing about installed patches.","Banner grabbing reads service version strings, which can be misleading or suppressed and do not show OS or back-ported patches."],2],["cs70",0,7,"During an incident, what is the FIRST priority?",["Preserving evidence","Ensuring the safety of people","Restoring operations","Notifying the media"],1,"Human life and safety always come first.","Outline 7.15",["Preserving evidence is important but always comes after protecting human life and safety.",null,"Restoring operations is a later recovery goal and never outranks the safety of people.","Media notification is handled later through the communications plan, if needed at all."],2],["cs71",0,7,"What is the PRIMARY purpose of chain of custody documentation?",["To speed up forensic analysis","To identify the attacker","To prove evidence has been handled properly and not altered","To reduce storage requirements"],2,"Chain of custody establishes integrity and admissibility of evidence.","Outline 7.1",["Chain of custody records who handled evidence and when; it does not make analysis faster.","Identifying the attacker is the goal of the investigation, not of the custody record.",null,"Chain of custody has nothing to do with storage efficiency."],1],["cs72",0,7,"Which order of volatility should a forensic investigator follow when collecting evidence?",["Disk, RAM, archived media, CPU cache","Archived media, disk, RAM, CPU cache","RAM, archived media, disk, CPU cache","CPU cache and registers, RAM, disk, archived media"],3,"Collect the most volatile data first because it is lost soonest.","Outline 7.1",["This starts with disk, the least volatile live source, so RAM and cache would be lost first.","This is exactly reversed: archived media is the least volatile source and should be collected last.","RAM should come early, but CPU cache is more volatile and is collected first, and archived media belongs at the end.",null],1],["cs73",0,7,"Which recovery site provides the fastest recovery but at the highest cost?",["Hot site","Warm site","Cold site","Mobile site"],0,"Hot sites are fully equipped with current data and can take over quickly.","Outline 7.10",[null,"A warm site has some equipment but needs data restoration and setup, so recovery is slower and cheaper than a hot site.","A cold site has only space, power and connectivity, so it is the cheapest and slowest option.","A mobile site is a transportable facility that must be deployed and configured, so it is not the fastest recovery option."],1],["cs74",0,7,"Which DR test runs the recovery site's systems while the primary site continues normal operation?",["Full interruption test","Parallel test","Read-through test","Walkthrough test"],1,"Parallel tests process real work at the alternate site without shutting down production.","Outline 7.12",["A full interruption test shuts down the primary site and shifts all work to the recovery site, the riskiest test type.",null,"A read-through (checklist) test only has participants review the plan documents; no systems are run.","A walkthrough is a group discussion of the plan's steps; no recovery systems process real work."],1],["cs75",0,7,"Which backup type copies all changes since the last full backup and does NOT reset the archive bit?",["Incremental","Full","Differential","Copy-on-write snapshot"],2,"Differential backups grow until the next full backup; restore needs the full plus the latest differential.","Outline 7.10",["An incremental backup copies changes since the last backup of any kind and does reset the archive bit.","A full backup copies everything, not just changes, and resets the archive bit.",null,"A copy-on-write snapshot is a point-in-time image mechanism, not an archive-bit-based backup type."],1],["cs76",0,7,"Job rotation is MOST effective at which of the following?",["Improving system performance","Reducing licensing costs","Preventing malware infections","Detecting fraud and collusion"],3,"Another person performing the role may uncover irregularities.","Outline 7.4",["Job rotation does not affect system performance; it is a personnel control.","Rotation does not reduce licensing costs and may even require more trained staff.","Job rotation does nothing to block malware; that needs technical controls.",null],2],["cs77",0,7,"A critical patch is released for a production database. What should the security manager do FIRST?",["Test the patch and apply it through the change management process","Install it immediately on all production servers","Wait for the next annual maintenance window","Disable the database until the vendor confirms stability"],0,"Patches should be tested and approved to avoid outages, with urgency matched to risk.","Outline 7.8",[null,"Installing an untested patch on all production servers risks outages and bypasses change control.","Waiting for an annual window leaves a critical vulnerability exposed far too long.","Disabling the database causes a self-inflicted outage; the risk should be handled through tested change management instead."],2],["cs78",0,7,"What is the MOST important output of the lessons-learned phase of incident response?",["A list of employees to discipline","Improvements to plans, controls and training to prevent recurrence","Deleting logs from the incident","A press release"],1,"Lessons learned exists to improve the program.","Outline 7.6",["Lessons learned is a blameless process to improve the program, not to single out employees for discipline.",null,"Incident logs must be retained as evidence and for analysis, not deleted.","Public communication is handled by the communications plan and is not the purpose of lessons learned."],2],["cs79",0,7,"Which tool automates response actions across security products based on playbooks?",["SIEM without automation","NetFlow collector","SOAR","Vulnerability scanner"],2,"SOAR orchestrates and automates response workflows.","Outline 7.2",["A SIEM without automation collects and correlates events but relies on analysts to take response actions.","A NetFlow collector records traffic metadata and does not run response playbooks.",null,"A vulnerability scanner finds weaknesses; it does not orchestrate response across security tools."],1],["cs80",0,7,"An employee traveling abroad is forced to open a secure facility under threat. Which control lets them signal this covertly?",["A mantrap","Security awareness training","Badge readers with PINs","A duress code or alarm"],3,"Duress signals alert security silently while appearing to comply.","Outline 7.15",["A mantrap controls entry to prevent tailgating but gives no way to signal distress covertly.","Awareness training teaches people what to do but is not a mechanism to send a silent signal.","A badge-and-PIN reader adds authentication, but a normal PIN gives no covert alert unless a separate duress code is configured.",null],2],["cs81",0,7,"Which is the BEST way to keep server configurations from drifting from approved settings?",["Automated configuration management against a documented baseline","Manual checks once a year","Allowing administrators to change settings as needed","Rebuilding servers only after incidents"],0,"Baselines with automated enforcement detect and correct drift.","Outline 7.3",[null,"Yearly manual checks leave drift undetected for months and are error-prone.","Letting administrators change settings freely is the cause of drift, not a control against it.","Rebuilding only after incidents is reactive and lets drift accumulate until something goes wrong."],2],["cs82",0,8,"When is the MOST cost-effective time to address security in the software development lifecycle?",["During user acceptance testing","During requirements and design","After deployment to production","During decommissioning"],1,"Defects found early are much cheaper to fix than later in the lifecycle.","Outline 8.1",["Fixing security flaws during user acceptance testing means reworking code that is already built, which costs far more than designing it in.",null,"Fixing flaws after deployment is the most expensive point, adding outages, patching and possible breach costs.","Decommissioning is the end of the lifecycle; security gaps have been exposed the whole time by then."],2],["cs83",0,8,"What is the BEST defense against SQL injection?",["Web application firewall only","Encrypting the database","Parameterized queries (prepared statements)","Hiding error messages"],2,"Parameterized queries keep data separate from code; WAFs are compensating controls.","Outline 8.5",["A WAF can filter many attack patterns but can be bypassed, so it is a compensating control, not a fix for vulnerable code.","Encrypting the database protects stored data but does not stop injected SQL from running through the application.",null,"Hiding error messages makes injection harder to discover but leaves the vulnerability in place, as blind SQL injection shows."],2],["cs84",0,8,"Which testing approach analyzes source code without executing it?",["Dynamic application security testing (DAST)","Penetration testing","Load testing","Static application security testing (SAST)"],3,"SAST reviews code at rest; DAST tests the running application.","Outline 8.2",["DAST tests the running application from the outside, so it executes the code.","Penetration testing attacks the running system and does not analyze source code at rest.","Load testing measures performance under heavy traffic by running the application, not by reviewing code.",null],1],["cs85",0,8,"An organization wants to find known-vulnerable open-source libraries in its applications. Which tool is BEST?",["Software composition analysis (SCA)","DAST scanner","Network vulnerability scanner","Fuzzer"],0,"SCA inventories third-party components and flags known vulnerabilities and license issues.","Outline 8.2",[null,"A DAST scanner tests a running app's behavior and does not inventory library versions against known CVEs.","A network vulnerability scanner checks hosts and services, not the open-source dependencies inside application code.","A fuzzer finds unknown crashes through malformed input rather than identifying known-vulnerable libraries."],1],["cs86",0,8,"Which attack tricks an authenticated user's browser into submitting an unwanted request to a trusted site?",["Cross-site scripting (XSS)","Cross-site request forgery (CSRF)","SQL injection","Buffer overflow"],1,"CSRF abuses the site's trust in the user's browser; anti-CSRF tokens mitigate it.","Outline 8.5",["XSS injects malicious script into pages viewed by users; it abuses the user's trust in the site rather than making the browser send forged requests.",null,"SQL injection targets the database through unsanitized input and does not rely on a victim's authenticated browser.","A buffer overflow writes past memory bounds in a program; it is not a browser-based forged request."],1],["cs87",0,8,"Before buying a commercial SaaS application for sensitive data, what should the security manager do FIRST?",["Run a penetration test on the vendor's production systems","Ask developers to review the vendor's source code","Assess the vendor's security posture and contractual commitments","Deploy the application to production to evaluate it"],2,"Acquired software risk is managed through vendor assessment and contracts; testing a vendor's systems needs their permission.","Outline 8.4",["Pen testing a vendor's production systems without their permission may be illegal and breach terms of service.","Commercial SaaS vendors rarely share source code, and reviewing it is not the first step in assessing acquired software.",null,"Putting sensitive data in an unvetted application in production accepts the risk before assessing it."],3],["cs88",0,8,"Why should developers NOT have access to deploy code directly into production?",["It improves code performance","It reduces the need for testing","It is required for agile methodologies","It enforces separation of duties and change control"],3,"Separating development from production prevents unauthorized or untested changes.","Outline 8.1",["Access controls on deployment have nothing to do with how fast the code runs.","Separating deployment from development does not reduce testing; it helps ensure testing actually happens.","Agile does not require developers to deploy directly to production; DevOps pipelines still enforce controlled releases.",null],3],["cs89",0,8,"Which maturity model is specifically designed to assess and improve an organization's software security practices?",["OWASP SAMM","ITIL","COBIT","ISO 9001"],0,"SAMM measures software assurance maturity and guides improvement.","Outline 8.1",[null,"ITIL is a framework for IT service management, not software security maturity.","COBIT is a governance and management framework for enterprise IT, not specific to software security practices.","ISO 9001 is a general quality management standard and does not address software security."],1],["cs90",0,8,"What is the BEST way to integrate security testing into a DevSecOps pipeline?",["Run a manual penetration test before each commit","Automate security checks as gates in the CI/CD pipeline","Test security only annually","Rely on production monitoring to find flaws"],1,"Automated gates give fast, consistent feedback without slowing delivery.","Outline 8.3",["Manual pen testing before every commit is far too slow and costly to scale in a CI/CD pipeline.",null,"Annual testing lets flaws reach production and linger for months, which defeats continuous delivery.","Relying on production monitoring finds flaws only after they are deployed and possibly exploited."],2],["x1",0,1,"A security team learns that a former employee has been posting the company's proprietary source code on a public forum. The company intends to sue for monetary damages. Which type of investigation is this?",["Criminal investigation","Regulatory investigation","Civil investigation","Administrative investigation"],2,"Seeking monetary damages from another party is a civil matter, decided on a preponderance of the evidence. A criminal investigation is brought by the government to punish lawbreaking, not to recover the company's losses.","Domain 1: Investigation types",["A criminal case is brought by the government to punish a crime, not by the company to recover money.","A regulatory investigation is run by or for a government regulator over compliance with rules, not a private damages claim.",null,"An administrative investigation is internal and deals with policy violations or operational issues, not a lawsuit for damages."],2],["x2",0,1,"A company bought a new encryption product six months ago. Auditors now ask for evidence that the product and its risks were researched and evaluated BEFORE it was purchased. Which concept are the auditors testing?",["Due diligence","Due care","Separation of duties","Risk transference"],0,"Due diligence is the research and investigation done before acting, so the organization knows what it is getting into. Due care is the ongoing responsible action afterward, such as operating and maintaining the control, which is not what the auditors asked about.","Domain 1: Due care vs due diligence",[null,"Due care is the ongoing responsible operation of a control after acting, not the research done before the purchase.","Separation of duties splits critical tasks among people; it says nothing about pre-purchase evaluation.","Risk transference shifts risk to a third party such as an insurer; it is not evidence of prior research."],2],["x3",0,1,"A startup has no reliable historical loss data but must rank its top risks for the board by next week. Which approach is MOST appropriate?",["Calculate annualized loss expectancy for every asset using guessed exposure factors","Wait until a full year of incident data has been collected","Purchase cyber insurance to cover all identified risks","Perform a qualitative assessment rating likelihood and impact on a scale"],3,"Qualitative analysis ranks risks on defined scales such as high, medium and low and suits situations with little reliable numeric data and a short deadline. Quantitative ALE figures built on guesses give false precision and take much longer.","Domain 1: Qualitative risk assessment",["ALE figures built on guessed exposure factors create false precision and take far longer than the deadline allows.","Waiting a year ignores the board's one-week deadline and leaves risks unranked in the meantime.","Insurance is a risk response, not a way to identify and rank risks, and rarely covers all of them.",null],3],["x4",0,1,"A document recommends, but does not require, that staff use a password manager and suggests several approved products. What type of document is this?",["Standard","Guideline","Procedure","Baseline"],1,"Guidelines are discretionary recommendations that help people meet policy goals. A standard would make a specific product or setting mandatory, which this document explicitly does not.","Domain 1: Policies, standards, procedures and guidelines",["A standard is mandatory and would require a specific product or setting, but this document only recommends.",null,"A procedure gives mandatory step-by-step instructions for doing a task, not optional suggestions.","A baseline sets a mandatory minimum security configuration, whereas this document is discretionary."],1],["x5",0,1,"A company's secret manufacturing formula gives it a competitive advantage. It wants protection that lasts as long as the formula stays confidential, without publicly disclosing it. Which protection fits BEST?",["Patent","Copyright","Trade secret","Trademark"],2,"Trade secret protection lasts as long as the owner takes reasonable steps to keep the information secret and requires no public disclosure. A patent requires publishing the invention and expires after a fixed term, which defeats the goal.","Domain 1: Intellectual property",["A patent requires public disclosure of the invention and expires after a fixed term.","Copyright protects the expression of a work, not the underlying formula or idea itself.",null,"A trademark protects names, logos and brand identifiers, not a manufacturing process."],2],["x6",0,1,"A new hire is about to be given access to confidential product plans. Which should be completed BEFORE access is granted?",["Background screening suited to the role and a signed NDA","An exit interview checklist and a company property return form","A job rotation schedule for the next twelve months","Enrollment in the annual security refresher course"],0,"Screening and nondisclosure agreements are hiring and onboarding controls that belong in place before sensitive access is granted. Exit interviews and property return are termination steps, not onboarding.","Domain 1: Personnel security",[null,"Exit interviews and property return belong to termination, not to onboarding before access is granted.","Job rotation is an ongoing operational control and does not vet the person before access.","An annual refresher happens later; initial screening and the NDA must come before access."],2],["x7",0,1,"A threat modeling team wants a seven-stage, risk-centric method that starts from business objectives and ends with attack simulation and risk and impact analysis. Which methodology fits?",["STRIDE","DREAD","OCTAVE","PASTA"],3,"PASTA (Process for Attack Simulation and Threat Analysis) is a seven-stage, risk-centric methodology that begins with business objectives. STRIDE is a scheme for classifying threats, not a staged business-driven process.","Domain 1: Threat modeling",["STRIDE is a threat categorization mnemonic, not a seven-stage risk-centric process.","DREAD is a scoring scheme for rating threats, not a staged methodology starting from business objectives.","OCTAVE is an organizational risk assessment approach, not the seven-stage attack-simulation method described.",null],2],["x8",0,1,"A CISSP learns that a colleague, also an ISC2 member, is falsely claiming to hold another certification in client proposals. What is the MOST appropriate action under the ISC2 Code of Ethics?",["Ignore it, since misrepresenting credentials is not a security breach","Report the conduct through the ISC2 ethics complaint process","Publicly post about the colleague on social media","Contact the client and cancel the proposal"],1,"Members must act honorably and advance and protect the profession, and ISC2 provides a formal complaint process for members' ethics violations. Ignoring it is tempting, but it tolerates dishonesty that harms clients and trust in the profession.","Domain 1: Professional ethics",["Ignoring it fails the canon to act honorably and advance the profession; credential fraud is an ethics issue.",null,"Public shaming on social media is not a proper or fair process and may itself be unprofessional.","Contacting the client and cancelling a proposal oversteps the member's authority and skips the formal process."],2],["x9",0,1,"A business impact analysis finds that the order-processing system has a maximum tolerable downtime (MTD) of 8 hours. What does this mean for its recovery time objective (RTO)?",["The RTO must be longer than 8 hours to allow testing","The RTO should equal the RPO for this system","The RTO must be less than or equal to 8 hours","The RTO is irrelevant once the MTD is known"],2,"The RTO is the target time to restore the system and must fit within the MTD, the point at which harm becomes unacceptable; in practice it is set below the MTD to leave margin. An RTO longer than the MTD would guarantee unacceptable harm.","Domain 1: Business impact analysis",["An RTO longer than the MTD would mean recovery finishes after unacceptable harm has already occurred.","RTO measures time to restore and RPO measures acceptable data loss; they are different and need not be equal.",null,"The MTD sets the upper limit that the RTO must fit within, so the RTO is still essential."],3],["x10",0,1,"Phishing simulations show the same click rate before and after a year of annual slide-based awareness training. What should the security manager do?",["Stop phishing simulations since they are upsetting staff","Discipline every employee who clicked a simulated link","Add more slides to the annual training session","Redesign the content and delivery, then measure again"],3,"Unchanged results show the program is not changing behavior, so its content and delivery need redesigning (for example shorter, more frequent, role-based training) and the effect remeasured. Adding more slides repeats the approach that already failed.","Domain 1: Security awareness program effectiveness",["Stopping simulations removes the measurement and does nothing to change user behavior.","Blanket discipline discourages reporting and does not fix training that is not working.","More slides in the same annual format repeats the approach the data already shows is ineffective.",null],2],["x11",0,2,"Which data role is responsible for the quality, definitions and metadata of a data set, ensuring it is fit for business use?",["Data steward","Data custodian","Data processor","Data subject"],0,"A data steward manages data quality, definitions and metadata so data is consistent and usable. A custodian implements technical protections such as backups and access controls but is not responsible for what the data means or its quality.","Domain 2: Data roles",[null,"A data custodian implements and maintains technical protection such as backups, not data quality and definitions.","A data processor handles personal data on behalf of a controller; it does not own data quality for the business.","The data subject is the person the data is about and has no responsibility for its quality or metadata."],1],["x12",0,2,"A hard drive that held sensitive data will be reused inside the same secure facility at the same sensitivity level. The data must not be recoverable with ordinary operating system or file recovery tools. Which sanitization level is the MINIMUM appropriate?",["Destroy","Clear","Purge","Quick format"],1,"Clearing overwrites user-addressable storage to defeat simple, non-invasive recovery with standard tools, which is adequate when media stays in the same protected environment. Purging defends against laboratory recovery and is needed when media leaves that control, so it exceeds the minimum here.","Domain 2: Data remanence and sanitization",["Destruction is excessive when the drive will be reused at the same sensitivity level inside the facility.",null,"Purge protects against laboratory recovery and is needed when media leaves the facility's control, which is not the case here.","A quick format leaves the data readable by common file recovery tools."],3],["x13",0,2,"Employees are uploading company files to unsanctioned cloud storage apps. The security team wants visibility into cloud app usage and the ability to enforce data policies on sanctioned SaaS apps. Which control BEST fits?",["Host-based firewall","Full-disk encryption","Network intrusion detection system","Cloud access security broker"],3,"A CASB sits between users and cloud services to discover shadow IT and enforce data security policies on SaaS use. A network IDS can flag suspicious traffic but cannot see into or enforce policy on encrypted SaaS sessions.","Domain 2: CASB",["A host-based firewall filters traffic on one host but cannot see or govern cloud app usage and data policies.","Full-disk encryption protects data at rest on a lost device, not uploads to unsanctioned cloud services.","A NIDS looks for attack signatures and cannot enforce data policies inside sanctioned SaaS apps.",null],3],["x14",0,2,"A publisher wants to stop recipients from printing or forwarding confidential reports even after the files have been downloaded to the recipients' own devices. Which control is MOST suitable?",["Transport Layer Security","Data classification labels","Digital rights management","File integrity monitoring"],2,"DRM (information rights management) attaches usage restrictions such as no print or no forward that travel with the file and are enforced wherever it is opened. Labels tell people how to handle data but do not technically stop printing.","Domain 2: DRM",["TLS protects data only in transit and does nothing once the file is on the recipient's device.","Labels tell people how to handle data but cannot technically stop printing or forwarding.",null,"File integrity monitoring detects changes to files; it does not restrict what recipients do with them."],2],["x15",0,2,"A vendor announces end of support for a firewall model that protects a critical network segment. What is the GREATEST security concern?",["Hardware faults will no longer be covered by the vendor's warranty","Newly found vulnerabilities will not receive security patches","The device's management interface will stop working","Existing firewall rules will be deleted automatically"],1,"Once support ends, newly discovered vulnerabilities go unpatched and the device stays exposed, so it should be replaced or protected with compensating controls. Loss of warranty is a cost issue, not the main security risk.","Domain 2: End-of-support assets",["Losing warranty coverage is an operational cost issue, less serious than unpatched security flaws.",null,"End of support does not make the management interface stop working.","Existing firewall rules keep working after end of support; they are not deleted."],2],["x16",0,2,"An organization adopts a security control baseline and then adjusts several control parameters, such as password length and lockout thresholds, to match its own risk assessment. What is this called?",["Tailoring","Scoping","Benchmarking","Accreditation"],0,"Tailoring modifies baseline controls, including their parameter values, to fit the organization's needs and risk. Scoping, the tempting alternative, decides which controls apply at all rather than how they are set.","Domain 2: Scoping and tailoring",[null,"Scoping removes baseline controls that do not apply, rather than adjusting the parameters of controls that do.","Benchmarking compares performance or configuration against a reference, not customizing a baseline.","Accreditation is formal management approval to operate a system, not adjusting control values."],3],["x17",0,3,"A banking application lets users change account balances only through certified programs that enforce well-formed transactions, never by editing records directly. Which security model does this reflect?",["Bell-LaPadula","Brewer-Nash","Clark-Wilson","Take-Grant"],2,"Clark-Wilson enforces integrity with the access triple: subjects can modify constrained data items only through transformation procedures that ensure well-formed transactions. Bell-LaPadula is a confidentiality model and says nothing about well-formed transactions.","Domain 3: Security models",["Bell-LaPadula protects confidentiality with read and write rules, not well-formed transactions.","Brewer-Nash prevents conflicts of interest between competing clients' data.",null,"Take-Grant models how rights are passed between subjects, not transaction integrity through certified programs."],2],["x18",0,3,"Under the Common Criteria, which document states the security requirements a group of consumers needs for a class of products, independent of any specific product?",["Protection Profile","Security Target","Target of Evaluation","Evaluation Assurance Level"],0,"A Protection Profile states implementation-independent security needs for a category of products. A Security Target is the vendor's security claims for one specific product, so it is not product-independent.","Domain 3: Common Criteria",[null,"A Security Target describes the security claims for one specific product being evaluated.","The Target of Evaluation is the product or system itself, not a requirements document.","An Evaluation Assurance Level is a rating of assurance depth, not a statement of consumer requirements."],1],["x19",0,3,"A certificate authority needs its root signing keys generated and used inside tamper-resistant hardware so they are never exported in plaintext. Which solution is BEST?",["A TPM chip on the administrator's laptop","An encrypted key file on a network share","A password-protected software key store","A hardware security module"],3,"An HSM is a dedicated, tamper-resistant device built for high-assurance key generation, storage and signing, which is why CAs use them for root keys. A TPM protects keys for its own host and is not meant to serve as a CA's signing appliance.","Domain 3: HSM",["A TPM protects one device's keys and is not designed for high-volume CA signing operations.","An encrypted key file on a share can be copied and attacked offline, so the key is exportable.","A software key store keeps the key in ordinary memory and storage, which is not tamper-resistant hardware.",null],3],["x20",0,3,"A company discovers that the private key used to sign its software updates may have been copied by an attacker. What should it do FIRST?",["Rotate all user passwords across the organization","Revoke the certificate and generate a new key pair","Increase the key length of the existing key","Delete the private key everywhere but keep the certificate valid"],1,"A suspected key compromise requires revoking the certificate so relying parties stop trusting it, then issuing a new key pair and re-signing. Deleting your copy of the key leaves the certificate trusted, so the attacker's copy can still sign malware.","Domain 3: Key management lifecycle",["User passwords are unrelated to the compromised code-signing key, so rotating them does not fix the problem.",null,"The existing key is already compromised; lengthening it is not possible and would not remove the attacker's copy.","If the certificate stays valid, the attacker can keep signing malware that will be trusted."],3],["x21",0,3,"An attacker who compromised one workstation extracts NTLM password hashes from memory and uses them to authenticate to other servers without cracking them. Which attack is this?",["Rainbow table attack","Birthday attack","Pass the hash","Brute-force attack"],2,"In pass the hash, the attacker presents the captured hash itself as the credential, so no cracking is needed. A rainbow table attack tries to recover the plaintext password from a hash, which the attacker never did here.","Domain 3: Cryptanalytic attacks",["Rainbow tables are used to crack hashes into passwords, but here the hashes are used without cracking.","A birthday attack finds hash collisions and is not about reusing stolen login hashes.",null,"Brute force guesses passwords repeatedly, whereas the attacker already has usable hashes."],2],["x22",0,3,"A development team runs its applications as containers on a shared host. Which risk is MOST specific to containers compared with virtual machines?",["All containers share the host's kernel","Container images cannot be scanned for flaws","Each container needs its own hypervisor","Containers cannot use encrypted connections"],0,"Containers isolate processes but share the host OS kernel, so a kernel vulnerability or container escape can expose every container on the host. Virtual machines each run their own kernel on a hypervisor, giving stronger isolation.","Domain 3: Container vulnerabilities",[null,"Container images can and should be scanned for vulnerabilities, so this is not a real limitation.","Containers share the host OS through a container runtime and do not each need a hypervisor.","Containers can use TLS and other encryption just like any other workload."],2],["x23",0,3,"A data center wants to prevent tailgating at its main entrance so that only one authenticated person enters at a time. Which control is MOST effective?",["CCTV cameras recorded to a DVR","A posted sign prohibiting tailgating","A badge reader on the outer door","A mantrap (access control vestibule)"],3,"A mantrap uses two interlocking doors so each person must authenticate and pass through a small space alone before the inner door opens, physically preventing tailgating. A badge reader alone lets a second person follow through the open door.","Domain 3: Facility access controls",["CCTV records tailgating after the fact but does not physically stop a second person entering.","A sign is only a deterrent and is easily ignored by someone determined to follow.","A badge reader authenticates one person but a follower can still slip through the open door.",null],2],["x24",0,3,"A server room's humidity sensor has shown very low humidity for several weeks. What is the PRIMARY risk to the equipment?",["Condensation forming on circuit boards","Electrostatic discharge damaging components","Corrosion of metal contacts","Overheating of power supplies"],1,"Low humidity lets static electricity build up, and electrostatic discharge can damage sensitive electronics. Condensation and corrosion are risks of humidity that is too high, not too low.","Domain 3: Environmental controls",["Condensation is a risk of high humidity, not the low humidity shown by the sensor.",null,"Corrosion is associated with high humidity and moisture, not dry air.","Low humidity does not directly cause power supplies to overheat; temperature is the concern there."],2],["x25",0,4,"A cloud provider needs far more isolated Layer 2 segments than the 802.1Q VLAN ID space allows, and those segments must stretch across a routed Layer 3 data center fabric. Which technology is designed for this?",["Private VLANs","Spanning Tree Protocol","VXLAN","Link aggregation"],2,"VXLAN encapsulates Layer 2 frames in UDP and uses a 24-bit network identifier, allowing millions of segments that span a routed fabric. Private VLANs subdivide a VLAN but are still bound by the 12-bit 802.1Q ID space and do not cross Layer 3.","Domain 4: VXLAN",["Private VLANs isolate ports within one VLAN but still use the limited 802.1Q ID space and do not span Layer 3.","Spanning Tree prevents Layer 2 loops and does not create additional segments.",null,"Link aggregation bundles physical links for bandwidth and redundancy, not more isolated segments."],2],["x26",0,4,"In a software-defined network, what is the key architectural change compared with traditional networking?",["Control and data planes are separated","Every switch is replaced with a firewall","All packets are encrypted by default","Routing is performed only by end hosts"],0,"SDN decouples the control plane (deciding where traffic goes) from the data plane (forwarding) and centralizes control in a programmable controller. SDN does not encrypt traffic by itself; confidentiality still needs separate controls.","Domain 4: SDN",[null,"SDN does not replace switches with firewalls; it centralizes control of forwarding devices.","SDN does not encrypt all packets by default; encryption is a separate choice.","Routing is still done by network devices, directed by a central controller, not by end hosts."],1],["x27",0,4,"Most of a company's data center traffic now flows between application servers rather than to and from the internet, and the perimeter firewall inspects none of it. Which traffic flow is the gap?",["North-south traffic","Out-of-band traffic","Broadcast traffic","East-west traffic"],3,"East-west traffic moves laterally between systems inside the data center and bypasses the perimeter firewall, which mainly sees north-south traffic entering and leaving. Micro-segmentation is a common way to close this gap.","Domain 4: Traffic flows",["North-south traffic enters and leaves the data center and is what the perimeter firewall already inspects.","Out-of-band traffic is management traffic on a separate network, not server-to-server application flows.","Broadcast traffic is one frame sent to all hosts in a segment, not the general flow between application servers.",null],3],["x28",0,4,"A network team monitors devices with SNMPv2c using community strings. What is the BEST improvement?",["Change the community string to a longer value","Migrate to SNMPv3 using authPriv mode","Move SNMP to a different UDP port number","Disable logging on the monitored devices"],1,"SNMPv3 with authPriv adds user-based authentication and encryption, while v2c community strings travel in clear text. A longer community string is still sent unencrypted and can be sniffed.","Domain 4: Secure protocols",["A longer community string is still sent in clear text and can be sniffed.",null,"Moving to another port is security through obscurity and leaves traffic unauthenticated and unencrypted.","Disabling logging reduces visibility and does nothing to secure SNMP."],2],["x29",0,4,"Users of a company's internal recursive DNS resolver are being sent to a fake banking site, even though the bank's authoritative DNS records are correct. What is the MOST likely attack?",["DNS tunneling","Domain hijacking at the registrar","DNS cache poisoning","A DNS amplification attack"],2,"Cache poisoning inserts forged records into a resolver's cache so its clients receive false answers even though the authoritative records are correct. Registrar hijacking would change the authoritative records themselves, which the stem rules out.","Domain 4: DNS attacks",["DNS tunneling hides data inside DNS queries to exfiltrate data or reach C2; it does not redirect users to fake sites.","Registrar hijacking would change the bank's authoritative records, but those are stated to be correct.",null,"DNS amplification is a reflection DDoS against a victim, not a way to redirect users."],3],["x30",0,4,"An attacker sends unsolicited messages to nearby phones over Bluetooth without accessing any data on them. What is this attack called?",["Bluejacking","Bluesnarfing","Bluebugging","War driving"],0,"Bluejacking sends unsolicited messages to discoverable Bluetooth devices and does not access data. Bluesnarfing is the tempting distractor, but it steals data such as contacts from the device.","Domain 4: Wireless networks",[null,"Bluesnarfing steals data from the device, but here no data was accessed.","Bluebugging takes control of the device's functions, far beyond sending unsolicited messages.","War driving is searching for wireless networks from a vehicle, not messaging phones over Bluetooth."],1],["x31",0,4,"A company moving its phone system to VoIP wants to protect both call signaling and voice content against eavesdropping. Which combination is MOST appropriate?",["Place the phones on the guest Wi-Fi network","Use SIP over UDP with RTP for media","Rely on the carrier's toll fraud monitoring","Use SIP over TLS with SRTP for media"],3,"SIP over TLS encrypts call signaling and SRTP encrypts the voice media stream, protecting both from eavesdropping. Plain SIP with RTP is the common default and sends signaling and audio unencrypted.","Domain 4: Converged protocols and VoIP",["A guest Wi-Fi network adds exposure and provides no encryption of signaling or voice.","Plain SIP over UDP and RTP send signaling and voice unencrypted, allowing eavesdropping.","Toll fraud monitoring detects misuse of billing, not eavesdropping on calls.",null],3],["x32",0,4,"An attacker on the same VLAN sends forged ARP replies so that traffic bound for the default gateway passes through the attacker's machine. Which switch feature BEST prevents this?",["Port mirroring","Dynamic ARP inspection","Spanning Tree Protocol","Jumbo frames"],1,"Dynamic ARP inspection validates ARP packets against trusted IP-to-MAC bindings, such as the DHCP snooping table, and drops forged replies. Port mirroring only copies traffic for monitoring and blocks nothing.","Domain 4: On-path attacks",["Port mirroring copies traffic for monitoring and does not block forged ARP replies.",null,"Spanning Tree prevents switching loops and has no ARP validation function.","Jumbo frames increase frame size for performance and have nothing to do with ARP spoofing."],2],["x33",0,5,"A hospital wants access decisions to consider the user's role and department, the patient's ward, the device being used and the time of day. Which access control model BEST supports this?",["Discretionary access control","Role-based access control","Attribute-based access control","Mandatory access control"],2,"ABAC evaluates policies built from attributes of the subject, object, action and environment, such as device and time. RBAC decides mainly on role membership, so adding context like location, device and time calls for attribute-based policies.","Domain 5: Authorization mechanisms",["DAC lets owners grant access at their discretion and cannot evaluate device, location and time conditions.","RBAC grants access by role alone and would need many roles to cover ward, device and time combinations.",null,"MAC uses labels and clearances set centrally, not dynamic contextual attributes like device and time."],3],["x34",0,5,"A user lets a photo printing website access photos stored at a cloud service without giving the printing site their password. Which protocol is designed for this?",["OAuth 2.0","Kerberos","RADIUS","LDAP"],0,"OAuth 2.0 is an authorization framework that issues access tokens so a third-party app can reach resources on the user's behalf without the user's password. Kerberos authenticates users inside an enterprise realm, not delegated web access between organizations.","Domain 5: Federated identity",[null,"Kerberos provides ticket-based authentication within a realm, not delegated access for third-party websites.","RADIUS is used for network access AAA, not letting one website access another on a user's behalf.","LDAP is a directory protocol and does not provide delegated authorization between web services."],2],["x35",0,5,"A company tunes the fingerprint readers on a high-security vault so that impostors are almost never accepted. What is the expected side effect?",["The crossover error rate drops to zero","The false acceptance rate increases","Enrollment takes less time for new users","The false rejection rate increases"],3,"Raising sensitivity lowers the false acceptance rate (Type II errors) but rejects more legitimate users, raising the false rejection rate (Type I errors). The two rates move in opposite directions; the CER is simply where they are equal.","Domain 5: Biometrics",["Tuning the threshold moves along the error curves; it cannot make the crossover error rate zero.","Making impostors almost never accepted lowers the false acceptance rate, not raises it.","The acceptance threshold does not shorten the enrollment process for new users.",null],3],["x36",0,5,"A bank lets customers open accounts online. Before issuing credentials, it verifies a government ID and matches a live selfie to the ID photo. What process is this?",["Authorization","Identity proofing","Accounting","Federation"],1,"Identity proofing verifies that a person is who they claim to be before an identity and credentials are created. Authorization comes later and decides what an authenticated identity may do.","Domain 5: Registration and proofing",["Authorization decides what an already identified user may do, not whether the claimed identity is real.",null,"Accounting records user activity for audit and billing, not verifying identity documents.","Federation shares identities across organizations, not verifying a new customer's documents."],2],["x37",0,5,"Logs show one failed login each for thousands of different accounts from the same source, all using the password 'Autumn2026!'. Which attack is this?",["Credential stuffing","Brute-force attack on a single account","Password spraying","Rainbow table attack"],2,"Password spraying tries one or a few common passwords across many accounts to stay under per-account lockout thresholds. Credential stuffing, the tempting option, replays username-password pairs leaked elsewhere, so the password would differ per account.","Domain 5: Access control attacks",["Credential stuffing reuses username and password pairs leaked from other sites, not one common password.","A brute-force attack on one account tries many passwords against that account, the reverse of this pattern.",null,"Rainbow tables crack stolen hashes offline and do not produce online login attempts."],2],["x38",0,5,"After several employees were phished for their one-time codes, a company wants phishing-resistant MFA. Which option is BEST?",["FIDO2 security keys or passkeys","SMS one-time passcodes","Push approval prompts that lack number matching","A password plus answers to personal security questions"],0,"FIDO2/WebAuthn authenticators cryptographically bind the login to the legitimate site's origin, so a fake site cannot capture a usable credential. SMS codes can be relayed by a phishing page in real time, which is exactly what happened.","Domain 5: MFA and passwordless",[null,"SMS codes can be phished or intercepted, the same weakness that caused the incidents.","Push prompts without number matching are open to MFA fatigue and relay attacks.","Security questions are another knowledge factor and can be researched or phished."],2],["x39",0,5,"A web application keeps users logged in indefinitely and does not change the session ID after login. Which change BEST reduces the risk of session hijacking?",["Require users to choose longer passwords when registering","Put session IDs in URLs so they are easier to track","Turn off TLS on internal pages to improve response time","New session ID at login plus idle timeouts"],3,"Regenerating the session ID at authentication prevents session fixation, and idle and absolute timeouts limit how long a stolen token is useful. Longer passwords do nothing once an attacker holds a valid session token.","Domain 5: Session management",["Longer passwords protect the login itself but not a session ID that has already been stolen or fixed.","Session IDs in URLs leak through logs, history and referrer headers, which increases hijacking risk.","Turning off TLS lets attackers sniff session cookies, making hijacking much easier.",null],3],["x40",0,5,"A login system allows access normally but demands extra verification when a user signs in from a new country or an unfamiliar device. What is this called?",["Discretionary access control","Risk-based (adaptive) authentication","Rule-based access control using a time-of-day rule","Mandatory access control"],1,"Risk-based authentication weighs signals such as location, device and behavior and steps up verification when risk is higher. A static time-of-day rule applies the same condition to everyone and does not assess each login's risk.","Domain 5: Risk-based access control",["DAC is an authorization model where owners grant access; it does not trigger extra authentication.",null,"A time-of-day rule is fixed and does not react to a new country or unfamiliar device.","MAC is a label-based authorization model and does not step up authentication based on risk."],2],["x41",0,6,"A security team wants to continuously and safely verify that its EDR and email controls detect current attack techniques, without waiting for the annual penetration test. Which approach BEST fits?",["Annual tabletop exercise","Manual code review","Breach and attack simulation","Credentialed vulnerability scanning"],2,"Breach and attack simulation tools run automated, safe emulations of real attack techniques on an ongoing basis to confirm that controls detect and block them. Vulnerability scanning finds missing patches and misconfigurations but does not test whether detections fire.","Domain 6: Breach and attack simulation",["A tabletop exercise is a discussion and does not technically test whether controls detect attacks.","Manual code review examines source code, not whether EDR and email controls detect techniques.",null,"Vulnerability scanning finds missing patches and misconfigurations but does not test detection of attack techniques."],3],["x42",0,6,"A company's external financial auditors want assurance about a payroll provider's controls that affect the company's financial statements. Which report should be requested?",["SOC 1","SOC 2","SOC 3","ISO 27001 certificate"],0,"SOC 1 reports cover a service organization's controls relevant to its customers' internal control over financial reporting, which is what financial auditors need. SOC 2 addresses the Trust Services Criteria such as security and availability, not financial reporting.","Domain 6: SOC reports",[null,"SOC 2 covers security and other trust services criteria, not controls over financial reporting.","SOC 3 is a general-use summary of SOC 2 and does not address financial reporting controls.","An ISO 27001 certificate covers the information security management system, not financial reporting controls."],2],["x43",0,6,"During requirements analysis, a team writes scenarios describing how an attacker might abuse the password reset feature so that tests can be built for them. What is this technique?",["Regression testing","Load testing","Synthetic transaction monitoring","Misuse case testing"],3,"Misuse (abuse) cases describe how a malicious user could exploit a feature and drive security tests for those scenarios. Regression testing only confirms that changes did not break existing functionality.","Domain 6: Misuse case testing",["Regression testing checks that changes did not break existing features, not attacker abuse scenarios.","Load testing measures performance under heavy use, not abuse of a feature by attackers.","Synthetic transactions monitor availability with scripted normal user actions, not attacker behavior.",null],2],["x44",0,6,"A startup's first SOC 2 audit evaluated whether its controls were suitably designed as of a specific date. What type of report is this?",["Type II","Type I","SOC 3","Agreed-upon procedures report"],1,"A Type I report assesses the design of controls at a point in time. A Type II report, the tempting answer, also tests whether controls operated effectively over a period, typically several months.","Domain 6: Type I vs Type II",["A Type II report evaluates operating effectiveness over a period of time, not design as of one date.",null,"SOC 3 is a general-use summary report and does not define whether design or operation is tested.","An agreed-upon procedures report gives findings on specific procedures and is not a SOC 2 design report."],1],["x45",0,6,"An online store's order, payment and inventory services each pass their own unit tests, but orders fail when data moves between them. Which testing BEST targets this problem?",["Static code analysis","Fuzz testing of the login page","Interface testing","Penetration testing of the perimeter"],2,"Interface testing verifies the exchanges between components, such as APIs and data formats, where these failures occur. Static analysis examines individual source files without running them, so it tends to miss runtime integration errors.","Domain 6: Interface testing",["Static analysis reviews code without running it and will not show failures when data passes between services.","Fuzzing the login page targets input handling on one component, not the data flow between services.",null,"A perimeter penetration test looks for external attack paths, not integration defects between services."],2],["x46",0,6,"A vulnerability scan finds a critical flaw on a legacy system that cannot be patched for six months because of a vendor dependency. What should happen?",["Approve a time-limited exception with compensating controls","Record the finding as a false positive so it stops appearing","Leave it unaddressed until the vendor issues a fix","Exclude the system from future vulnerability scans"],0,"When remediation is not possible, the risk owner should approve a documented, time-limited exception with compensating controls such as isolation and extra monitoring. Marking it a false positive falsifies the record and hides a real risk from management.","Domain 6: Exception handling",[null,"Marking a real flaw as a false positive falsifies records and hides the risk from management.","Leaving it unaddressed for six months without compensating controls accepts unmanaged risk.","Excluding the system from scans removes visibility into it and any new flaws."],3],["x47",0,6,"The CISO must show the board whether the security program is improving. Which is the BEST approach?",["Share the raw firewall and IDS logs from the last quarter","List every tool the team has purchased","Report the total number of alerts generated","Present trends of a few metrics tied to business risk"],3,"Boards need a small set of meaningful metrics, such as KRIs and KPIs, shown as trends and linked to business risk. Alert counts are volume measures that do not show whether risk is going down.","Domain 6: Security process data and metrics",["Raw logs are too detailed for a board and do not show whether risk is improving.","A list of tools shows spending, not whether the program is reducing risk.","Alert counts are an activity measure and can rise or fall for reasons unrelated to improvement.",null],3],["x48",0,7,"SIEM correlation rules miss an insider who slowly downloads unusual amounts of data at odd hours using valid credentials. Which capability would MOST likely detect this?",["Signature-based antivirus","User and entity behavior analytics","A stateful packet-filtering firewall","Static application security testing"],1,"UEBA builds baselines of normal behavior for users and devices and flags deviations such as unusual volumes or times, even when credentials are valid. Signature-based antivirus looks for known malicious code, and no malware is involved here.","Domain 7: UEBA",["Signature-based antivirus looks for known malware, not unusual behavior by a valid user.",null,"A stateful firewall allows legitimate sessions from valid users and cannot spot behavioral anomalies.","SAST analyzes application source code and has no view of user activity."],2],["x49",0,7,"A SOC analyst starts from a hypothesis that attackers may be using a newly reported persistence technique and searches endpoint data for signs of it, although no alert has fired. What is this activity?",["Incident triage","Vulnerability scanning","Threat hunting","Penetration testing"],2,"Threat hunting is proactive, hypothesis-driven searching for threats that evaded existing detections. Incident triage is reactive and starts from an alert, which is absent here.","Domain 7: Threat hunting",["Triage starts from an alert or incident, but here no alert has fired.","Vulnerability scanning looks for weaknesses in systems, not signs of attacker activity.",null,"Penetration testing is an authorized simulated attack, not a search for real attackers already present."],2],["x50",0,7,"A company tests its DR plan by gathering the recovery team in a meeting room to talk through a realistic outage scenario step by step, without touching production or recovery systems. Which test type is this?",["Read-through (checklist) test","Parallel test","Full interruption test","Structured walkthrough (tabletop)"],3,"In a structured walkthrough, or tabletop, the team meets and talks through a scenario and their roles without affecting systems. A read-through is less interactive: people individually review the plan documents for accuracy.","Domain 7: DR plan testing",["A read-through test is done by people reviewing the plan individually, not a group scenario discussion.","A parallel test actually runs recovery systems alongside production, but here no systems are touched.","A full interruption test shuts down the primary site, the opposite of this discussion-only exercise.",null],2],["x51",0,7,"Email attachments from outside the company should be opened in an isolated environment and observed for malicious behavior before users receive them. Which control does this describe?",["Sandboxing","Honeypot","Allow list","Data loss prevention"],0,"A sandbox runs untrusted files in an isolated environment and watches their behavior to detect malware before delivery. A honeypot is a decoy system meant to attract attackers, not to inspect inbound attachments.","Domain 7: Detective and preventive measures",[null,"A honeypot is a decoy to attract attackers, not a place to detonate incoming attachments.","An allow list permits only approved items and does not observe attachment behavior.","DLP stops sensitive data leaving the organization and does not analyze inbound attachments for malware."],2],["x52",0,7,"An analyst confirms that a server is communicating with a known command-and-control address, and evidence must be preserved. What is the BEST immediate action?",["Power off the server right away","Network-isolate it but leave it powered on","Wipe and reimage the server","Notify all customers that a breach has occurred"],1,"Network isolation contains the threat by cutting off command-and-control while preserving volatile evidence such as memory and running processes. Powering off is tempting because it stops the activity, but it destroys that volatile evidence.","Domain 7: Incident response and evidence",["Powering off destroys volatile evidence in memory such as running processes and network connections.",null,"Wiping destroys all evidence before the incident has been investigated.","Notifying customers before the scope is known is premature and may be inaccurate."],3],["x53",0,7,"A company takes a full backup every Sunday night and an incremental backup every other night. The server fails on Thursday afternoon. What is needed to restore it?",["Only Sunday's full backup","The Sunday full backup together with only the Wednesday incremental","Sunday's full plus Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday incrementals","Only Wednesday's incremental backup"],2,"Each incremental holds only the changes since the previous backup, so the full backup plus every incremental since it must be restored in order. Using only the latest backup after the full is how differential backups work, which is the tempting mix-up.","Domain 7: Backup types",["The full backup alone misses all changes made Monday through Wednesday.","Each incremental holds only changes since the previous backup, so Monday and Tuesday changes would be lost.",null,"An incremental backup cannot be restored without the full backup it builds on."],3],["x54",0,7,"A database administrator holds Top Secret clearance but is denied access to a Top Secret project file unrelated to their duties. Which principle is being enforced?",["Separation of duties","Job rotation","Dual control","Need to know"],3,"Need to know restricts access to the information a person requires for their job, even when their clearance is high enough. Separation of duties splits a critical task among people, which is not what is happening here.","Domain 7: Foundational operations concepts",["Separation of duties splits a task between people so no one can complete it alone, not limiting access by job need.","Job rotation moves staff between roles to detect fraud; it does not explain denying this file.","Dual control requires two people together to perform an action, which is not what is happening.",null],3],["x55",0,8,"An application accepts serialized objects from clients and rebuilds them on the server without validation. An attacker sends a crafted object that runs commands on the server. Which weakness is this?",["Insecure deserialization","Cross-site scripting","Clickjacking","Directory traversal"],0,"Insecure deserialization occurs when untrusted serialized data is turned back into objects without validation, which can lead to remote code execution. Cross-site scripting runs script in a victim's browser rather than code on the server.","Domain 8: Common weaknesses",[null,"XSS injects script into pages viewed by other users rather than running commands on the server from serialized data.","Clickjacking tricks users into clicking hidden page elements and does not run server commands.","Directory traversal reads files outside intended folders using path sequences, not crafted objects."],2],["x56",0,8,"A privileged program checks that a user may write to a file, then opens it a moment later. In between, an attacker swaps the file for a link to a sensitive system file. What is this vulnerability?",["Stack buffer overflow","TOCTOU race condition","Integer overflow","SQL injection"],1,"A time-of-check to time-of-use (TOCTOU) race exploits the gap between verifying a condition and acting on it. A buffer overflow writes past the end of a memory buffer, which is not what happened here.","Domain 8: Race conditions",["A buffer overflow writes past the end of memory, but here the file is swapped between check and use.",null,"An integer overflow is an arithmetic wraparound error, not a timing gap exploited by swapping a file.","SQL injection inserts code into database queries and is unrelated to file access timing."],2],["x57",0,8,"A web forum displays user comments. Testers find that a comment containing a script tag executes in other users' browsers. What is the BEST fix?",["Require HTTPS on every page","Switch to parameterized database queries","Encode output for the page context","Add a CAPTCHA to the comment form"],2,"Stored XSS is prevented by context-aware output encoding, backed by input validation, so user content is rendered as text instead of executed. Parameterized queries stop SQL injection, not scripts running in browsers.","Domain 8: Secure coding practices",["HTTPS encrypts traffic but the malicious script would still be delivered and run in browsers.","Parameterized queries stop SQL injection, not scripts executing in other users' browsers.",null,"A CAPTCHA slows automated posting but does not stop a person posting a malicious script."],2],["x58",0,8,"A team wants security testing that runs while functional tests exercise the application, using agents inside the running application to see both code paths and runtime data. Which approach fits?",["SAST","SCA","Manual code review","IAST"],3,"Interactive application security testing (IAST) instruments the running application, combining code-level insight with runtime behavior during testing. SAST analyzes code without running it, so it lacks runtime visibility.","Domain 8: Application security testing",["SAST analyzes code without running it, so it lacks runtime data.","SCA identifies vulnerable third-party components, not flaws observed while the app runs.","Manual code review is static and cannot observe runtime behavior during functional tests.",null],3],["x59",0,8,"A company depends on a small vendor's custom software for a critical process and worries the vendor could go out of business, leaving the product unsupported. Which contractual control BEST addresses this?",["Source code escrow","A non-disclosure agreement","A service level agreement for uptime","Annual penetration testing of the software"],0,"Source code escrow places the code with a third party that releases it to the customer if the vendor fails, so the software can still be maintained. An SLA sets performance expectations but cannot be enforced against a vendor that no longer exists.","Domain 8: Acquired software",[null,"An NDA protects confidential information but does not give access to the code if the vendor fails.","An SLA cannot be enforced against a vendor that no longer exists.","Penetration testing finds security flaws but does nothing about a vendor ceasing support."],3],["x60",0,8,"A mobile app's API returns a customer's order when given an order number. Testers find that changing the number in the request returns other customers' orders. What is the root cause?",["No TLS encryption between the mobile app and the API","Missing server-side authorization checks","Weak password complexity requirements","No rate limiting on the login endpoint"],1,"This is broken object-level authorization (an insecure direct object reference): the server must verify that the requester may access each object. TLS protects data in transit but does not stop an authenticated user from requesting other people's records.","Domain 8: API security",["TLS protects data in transit but does not stop an authenticated user requesting other customers' orders.",null,"Password complexity does not matter here because the user is already legitimately logged in.","Login rate limiting slows password guessing but does not control access to individual order records."],3]]);
